rcle cx="718" cy="580" r="17" fill="#C85A32" stroke="#FFFFFF" stroke-width="3"/><text x="718" y="586" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#FFFFFF" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text x="750" y="566" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">Up the walls</text><text x="750" y="594" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">A tall, narrow shelf in the corner</text><text x="750" y="616" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">uses height, not floor space</text><rect x="680" y="652" width="24" height="18" fill="url(#dshh)" stroke="#8FA689"/><text x="716" y="667" font-size="16" fill="#6E645C">walkway to keep clear (queen bed)</text><rect x="60" y="720" width="1080" height="110" rx="14" fill="#8FA689"/><text x="600" y="762" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#FFFFFF" text-anchor="middle">Tip: leave 24 in beside a queen bed and 30 in at the foot</text><text x="600" y="796" font-size="18" fill="#FFFFFF" text-anchor="middle">If storage furniture eats into these walkways, move it under the bed, onto the wall or behind the door.</text><text x="600" y="868" font-size="15" fill="#6E645C" text-anchor="middle">Clearances based on designer guidance published by Homes &amp; Gardens. Plan not to scale.</text></svg><figcaption style="text-align:center;font-size:0.9em;color:#6E645C;">Plan your storage around the walkways, not the other way around.</figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">How much space should you keep clear? In a guide to bedroom clearances from <a href="https://www.homesandgardens.com/interior-design/bedrooms/an-expert-guide-to-bedroom-clearances-measurements-and-spacing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Homes &amp; Gardens</a>, interior designers recommend about 24 to 30 inches on each side of a queen bed and 30 to 36 inches at the foot, with 18 inches as the bare minimum beside a twin or full bed. If you can&#8217;t keep those walkways with a dresser or chair in the room, it&#8217;s a sign that storage should move under the bed, onto the walls or behind the door.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Quick Pick: Small Bedroom Storage Ideas by Problem</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Your problem</th><th>Start with these ideas</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No room for a dresser</td><td>Storage bed (#1), under-bed bins (#2), closet drawers (#16)</td></tr><tr><td>Cluttered nightstand</td><td>Narrow nightstand (#5), floating nightstand (#6), wall lamps (#8)</td></tr><tr><td>Nowhere for bags and jackets</td><td>Over-the-door hooks (#13), wall hooks (#14)</td></tr><tr><td>Blankets and bedding everywhere</td><td>Storage bench (#10), under-bed bins (#2)</td></tr><tr><td>Floor space is gone</td><td>Shelves above the bed (#7), tall narrow shelf (#12)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Organize a Small Bedroom in 5 Steps</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Clear the floor first.</strong> Pick up everything that doesn&#8217;t belong on the floor and sort it into keep, move and donate.</li>



<li><strong>Move what doesn&#8217;t belong.</strong> Bedrooms collect laundry, paperwork and things from other rooms. Take them where they live.</li>



<li><strong>Measure your walkways.</strong> Check the space beside and at the foot of the bed before adding any furniture.</li>



<li><strong>Store up, under and behind.</strong> Use the bed, the walls and the door before you buy another piece of floor furniture.</li>



<li><strong>Give everything a home.</strong> Baskets, drawers and hooks only work if every item has one place to go.</li>
</ol>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">18 Small Bedroom Storage Ideas</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">These ideas are grouped by where the storage goes, starting with the biggest space most people ignore: under the bed.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Under the Bed</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-02-storage-bed.jpg" alt="Platform storage bed with two large drawers pulled out holding folded sweaters and spare bedding" class="wp-image-879" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-02-storage-bed.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-02-storage-bed-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-02-storage-bed-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-02-storage-bed-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">1. Choose a storage bed</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A platform bed with built-in drawers can replace a dresser in a small room. Drawers are easier to use than bins because they slide out smoothly and keep dust off your things.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2. Use low rolling bins</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you already have a bed you like, low bins on wheels turn the space underneath into storage. Use them for spare bedding, shoes or off-season clothes.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">3. Add bed risers if you need more height</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sturdy bed risers lift the frame a few inches so taller bins fit underneath. Choose a bed skirt or a neat set of matching bins so the space still looks tidy.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4. Keep a system under the bed</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Give each bin one job, like sweaters in one and sheets in another, and label the front. Under-bed storage only works if you don&#8217;t have to dig.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Nightstands and Bedside Storage</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-04-narrow-nightstand.jpg" alt="Narrow two-drawer bedside table with a woven basket underneath and a small floating shelf above it" class="wp-image-878" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-04-narrow-nightstand.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-04-narrow-nightstand-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-04-narrow-nightstand-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-04-narrow-nightstand-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">5. Pick a narrow nightstand with drawers</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A slim bedside table with two drawers hides small items and takes much less floor space than a wide one. A basket underneath adds extra storage for books or chargers.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">6. Try a floating nightstand</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A wall-mounted shelf or small floating drawer next to the bed keeps the floor open, which makes the room look bigger and easier to clean.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Walls and Shelves</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-03-shelves-above-bed.jpg" alt="Wooden floating shelves above a bed with books, baskets and plants, and wall-mounted reading lamps on both sides" class="wp-image-877" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-03-shelves-above-bed.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-03-shelves-above-bed-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-03-shelves-above-bed-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-03-shelves-above-bed-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">7. Hang shelves above the bed</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The wall above the headboard is often empty. One or two floating shelves can hold books, baskets and plants. Keep heavy items off shelves directly over your pillow. If you want to install your own, our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/diy-floating-shelves/">DIY floating shelves guide</a> shows how to hang them securely.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">8. Use wall-mounted lamps</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Plug-in wall sconces free up the whole top of your nightstand. They&#8217;re one of the easiest small bedroom storage ideas because they give back space you use every day.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9. Add a picture ledge for books</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A narrow ledge holds books and small decor without the depth of a full bookcase, which is ideal when the bed sits close to the wall.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Foot of the Bed</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-05-storage-bench.jpg" alt="Upholstered sage storage bench at the foot of a bed with the lid open showing folded blankets inside" class="wp-image-876" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-05-storage-bench.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-05-storage-bench-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-05-storage-bench-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-05-storage-bench-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">10. Add a storage bench</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A storage bench at the foot of the bed gives you a place to sit and a hidden spot for blankets and extra pillows. Before you buy one, make sure it still leaves a clear walkway at the foot of the bed.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">11. Use a blanket basket</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If a bench doesn&#8217;t fit, a tall woven basket in a corner holds throws and extra blankets and takes up very little floor space.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Doors, Corners and Walls</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-06-door-wall-storage.jpg" alt="Small bedroom corner with an over-the-door hook rack holding bags and a robe, a tall narrow shelf with baskets and a pegboard with hats" class="wp-image-875" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-06-door-wall-storage.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-06-door-wall-storage-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-06-door-wall-storage-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-06-door-wall-storage-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">12. Put a tall, narrow shelf in the corner</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A slim bookcase or ladder shelf uses height instead of floor space. Baskets on the lower shelves hide small items, and the top shelves can hold things you use less often.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">13. Use the back of the door</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">An over-the-door hook rack is perfect for bags, robes and tomorrow&#8217;s outfit. It keeps them off the bed and the chair, where clutter usually starts.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">14. Add wall hooks or a pegboard</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A row of hooks or a small pegboard by the door holds hats, bags and jewelry and turns everyday items into part of the decor.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Closets and Clothes</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">15. Double the hanging space</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Add a second rod below the first for shirts and folded pants. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-closet-organization-ideas/">small closet organization ideas</a> cover more ways to fit a lot into a reach-in closet.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">16. Put a dresser inside the closet</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If the closet has room, a small dresser or set of drawers inside it can replace a dresser in the bedroom and free up a whole wall.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">17. Store off-season clothes elsewhere</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Only keep this season&#8217;s clothes in the bedroom. Winter coats or summer clothes can go in labeled bins under the bed or on a high closet shelf.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">18. Keep bedding out of the bedroom</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Spare sheets and blankets don&#8217;t have to live in a small bedroom. If you have one, a tidy <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/linen-closet-organization/">linen closet</a> frees up drawers and under-bed space for clothes.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-07-before-after.jpg" alt="Small bedroom before and after: clothes piled on a chair and boxes on the floor next to the same room organized with a storage bed and shelves" class="wp-image-874" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-07-before-after.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-07-before-after-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-07-before-after-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/small-bedroom-storage-07-before-after-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The before-and-after above is the same small bedroom. Nothing was added to the floor; the storage moved under the bed, onto the wall and into drawers, and the room instantly feels bigger.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common Small Bedroom Storage Mistakes</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Blocking the walkways.</strong> A dresser or chair that makes you squeeze past the bed makes the room feel smaller.</li>



<li><strong>Buying storage before decluttering.</strong> Sort first, then buy only what you need.</li>



<li><strong>Wasting the space under the bed.</strong> It&#8217;s often the biggest storage area in the room.</li>



<li><strong>Too many open shelves.</strong> Lots of small items on display look cluttered fast. Use baskets.</li>



<li><strong>Letting the chair become a closet.</strong> Add hooks or a door rack for clothes you&#8217;ll wear again.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Small Bedroom Storage FAQ</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How can I add storage to a small bedroom without furniture?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Use the space under the bed, the wall above the headboard, the back of the door and the inside of the closet. Floating shelves, hooks and under-bed bins add storage without taking up floor space.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How much space should be around a bed?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Designers generally recommend about 24 to 30 inches on each side of a queen bed and 30 to 36 inches at the foot, with 18 inches as a minimum beside smaller beds.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Is a storage bed worth it for a small bedroom?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Yes, if you need to replace a dresser. Built-in drawers hold a lot, keep dust out and free up floor space for walking.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Where do you put clothes in a small bedroom with no closet?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Use a storage bed, a narrow dresser, a slim clothing rack against one wall and over-the-door hooks. Keep only the current season&#8217;s clothes in the room.</p>



